findings.claim:
The CIR lifetime checker (LifetimeCheck.cpp, 2344 lines; clang-tidy/cir/Lifetime.cpp) exists only in the closed incubator. It is a hand-written walker over structured CIR ops (IfOp, SwitchOp, LoopOpInterface, ScopeOp) with pset remarks and does not use MLIR's DataFlowSolver (0 occurrences). Main has no lifetime pass in CIR Passes.td and no clang-tidy/cir directory.

Main does add a CIR alias analysis (CIRBasicAliasAnalysis):
- offset-based, walking cir.get_member layout offsets;
- NoAlias only for distinct cir.alloca;
- 'TODO: Extend to cover global addresses, function arguments with noalias, and heap allocations';
- cir.call (CIR_CallOpBase) does not declare MemoryEffectOpInterface, so getModRef returns ModAndRef for calls.

CIR keeps member names (`cir.get_member %0[1] {name = "i"}`) and structured cir.if/switch/scope/while/for. Separately, upstream Clang's new LifetimeSafety analysis is built on the Clang CFG/AST, not CIR.

Experiment with upstream LocalAliasAnalysis, the only built-in alias analysis implementation:
- distinct globals @g_state vs @g_other (via llvm.mlir.addressof): MayAlias;
- &g_state.att vs &g_state.valid: MustAlias, because GEP implements ViewLikeOpInterface and is unwrapped to its base, so the analysis is field-insensitive;
- alloca vs global: NoAlias;
- call result or argument vs anything: MayAlias.
ModRef experiment: a store to .att is 'Mod' for the .valid location, and an external `llvm.call @CFE_ES_PerfLogAdd` is 'ModRef' for every location. llvm.call declares CallOpInterface but not MemoryEffectsOpInterface, unchanged on main. Main adds one relevant change: effects on non-addressable resources are NoAlias in getModRef.

Experiment with a YAML taint config (Propagations: CFE_SB_ReceiveBuffer DstArgs [0]; Sinks: Control Args [0]) and optin.taint.GenericTaint, local clang 1053047a:
- int payload: reported both directly (Control(buf->att)) and via a global within one function path;
- the same code with a double payload: no report;
- cross-handler run loop (handler 1 stores to the global, handler 2 reads it on a later iteration): no report, whether the global is static or not;
- isolated cause: any opaque call between store and read (Opaque(), or a second CFE_SB_ReceiveBuffer) drops the taint even for a `static` global; only the no-intervening-call case reports (inval.c:7).
cFS apps keep state in non-static globals, e.g. hk_app.c:42 `HK_AppData_t HK_AppData;`. CSA also supports CTU analysis and YAML taint configuration (docs).

PhASAR (HEAD 41d97dab, 2026-09-30):
- LLVM 16 to 22.1;
- IFDS/IDE/WPDS solvers, call graphs (CHA/RTA/VTA/alias-based), alias sets with optional SVF integration, a taint configuration, and a user-supplied entry-point list;
- the README mentions no concurrency support.

SVF (HEAD 6a4bb08f, 2026-10-01):
- LLVM up to 22;
- field- and flow-sensitive points-to, memory SSA, demand-driven analysis, abstract execution, and MTA (multithreaded value-flow, MHP, lock analysis, MSli slicing);
- thread APIs are a hard-coded table in ThreadAPI.cpp (pthread_create -> TD_FORK, pthread_mutex_lock and sem_wait -> TD_ACQUIRE).

Joern (HEAD 98a815cb, 2026-09-30):
- the c2cpg frontend is Eclipse-CDT based, accepts compile_commands.json, and emits problem nodes for unparsed code;
- builds code property graphs queried in a Scala DSL.

Frama-C:
- The official repo and site (git.frama-c.com, frama-c.com) are blocked here. The GitHub snapshot repo is frozen at 2020-10-21.
- opam has frama-c 33.0 'Arsenic' (LGPL-2.1). Its description lists Eva (abstract interpretation), WP, Slicing, Aoraï (automata-based properties) and E-ACSL, with no concurrency plugin listed.
- RacerF (ECOOP 2025, Frama-C plugin, pthread data races, heuristic) is known from snippets.

IKOS (HEAD ac7f7c17, 2025-04-23):
- needs LLVM/Clang 14; checks include boa, dbz, nullity and uva (uninitialized reads);
- its 'multi-threading' means parallel analysis jobs (`--jobs`);
- 'IKOS analyses each entry point independently, as if they were running in different processes.'

Goblint (HEAD d0ee7d5b, 2026-09-30):
- thread-modular abstract interpretation for C with race analysis and pthread semantics hard-coded in libraryFunctions.ml;
- an `ana.thread.wrappers` option for pthread_create wrappers;
- its OSEK and ARINC653 support 'has been removed from recent Goblint versions, please use Release 1.1.1'.

VAST:
- trailofbits/vast, HEAD 60d605d0, 2026-02-13, LLVM 19; an MLIR 'tower of IRs' frontend for C/C++ program analysis;
- the hl dialect keeps field names (`hl.member` with MemberVarSymbolRefAttr), typedefs and indirect calls;
- `vast-detect-parsers` lifts C into a domain 'parser dialect' guided by YAML function models, an architectural precedent for a cfs lifting with API models.

Macroni (last commit 2024-07-02) adds macro expansions to VAST and needs a PASTA-specific clang.

PoTATo:
- Jezurko/potato, last commit 2026-03-25: dialect-based points-to (Andersen/Steensgaard) on the MLIR DataFlow framework, with `--llvm-ir-to-potato`; the README says it is tested with LLVM 19 and was shown as a EuroLLVM 2024 poster;
- my builds of commits 80d157c and 20e7d8f against the local LLVM 1053047a failed with RegionSuccessor/DataFlow API errors.

SYCL-MLIR (Tiotto et al., CGO 2024): a domain dialect modelling SYCL runtime semantics for host/device analysis. Its source contains sycl::AliasAnalysis subclassing LocalAliasAnalysis with domain rules.

What a cfs dialect adds over an off-the-shelf tool with an SB model:
1. cFS API calls become typed ops whose operands (MID, pipe, buffer, status, timeout) and verifiers make missing return checks or unknown MIDs explicit.
2. Effects go on non-addressable resources (pipe/MID queue, table buffer, ES state), so modelled calls stop clobbering all C memory. Upstream llvm.call is ModRef-all (rw-mlir-08) and main's LocalAliasAnalysis treats non-addressable effects as NoAlias.
3. App/task/run-loop structure becomes regions, so inter-task reasoning is separate from intra-task dataflow.
4. Reuse of the DataFlowSolver (sparse/dense, DCA, interprocedural walk).
5. Location-carrying diagnostics: FileLineColLoc is preserved on calls and loads (rw-mlir-04).

What it costs (all researcher-built):
- a field-sensitive, global-aware memory model and alias analysis: upstream is field-insensitive and globals MayAlias (rw-mlir-08/09);
- a closed-world visibility fix (rw-mlir-07) and indirect-call resolution (rw-mlir-10);
- MID constant recovery and field-name and macro mapping (rw-mlir-05);
- an SB model, which every tool needs anyway (rw-mlir-12);
- a cross-task HB/interleaving layer, which MLIR does not provide (rw-mlir-06);
- tracking an unstable MLIR analysis API (rw-mlir-18);
- a frontend decision: the LLVM path works now; CIR is opt-in and untested here (rw-mlir-01/02).
